Suppose that whenever $|z-z'|_1\leq1$, and for every pair of labels in question, there is an $\mathcal F_{Q_H(z)}$-measurable variable $E_{(z,\lambda),(z',\lambda')}$ such that $$\left\|E_{(z,\lambda),(z',\lambda')}
       -V_{z',\lambda'}+V_{z,\lambda}\right\|_1
 \leq\varepsilon.                              \label{found:eq-abstract-hyp}$$

It must satisfy $$\label{access:slab}
 \begin{split}
 D_0&\subset [-C K,C K]^2+z,\\
 D_0&\subset
 \{\xi:\ h(c_--z)-b_0\le h(\xi-z)\le h(c_+-z)+b_0\},
 \end{split}$$

outputs.exact_statement:
For $\tau\sim\operatorname{Gamma}(\kappa,\lambda)$ and $\theta<\lambda$, $$\mathbb Ee^{\theta\tau}
 =\left(\frac{\lambda}{\lambda-\theta}\right)^\kappa.$$ If $V$ is the sum over a deterministic set of $M$ distinct edges and $M\le A v$, where $A,v>0$, then $$\label{df:eq:gamma-sum-tail}
 \mathbb P\left(V>\frac{2A\kappa}{\lambda}v\right)
 \le \exp\{-A\kappa(1-\log 2)v\}.$$

The linear functionals $f_-$, $f_+$, and $f$ are bounded above by $\mu$ on $\mathbb R^2$ and equal $\mu$ at $u$. For $\sigma\in\{-,+\}$, with the corresponding numerical sign when multiplied by a scalar, $$\label{df:eq:support-contact}
 \mu(u+te_2)-f_\sigma(u+te_2)=o(|t|)
 \qquad(t\to0,\ \sigma t>0).$$

Fix $A,R,\varepsilon>0$ and $\sigma\in\{-,+\}$. As $s\to\infty$ and $n/s\to\infty$, with $s$ real and $n$ a positive integer, $$\label{df:eq:cr-rare-fast}
 \mathbb P\left(
 \begin{array}{l}
 \text{there is a path $P$ from $x=0$ to $x=n$ with}
       \ H_\sigma(P)<-\varepsilon s,\\
 \text{all of whose vertices satisfy }
       -An\le x\le(1+A)n,\quad |r|\le Rs
 \end{array}\right)\longrightarrow0.$$ The same assertion holds, with the same probability, for every exact lattice translate of the event.

For every fixed $C>0$, $$\label{df:eq:gr-short-crossings}
 \mathbb P(E(s,n))\longrightarrow1
 \qquad\text{as }s\to\infty,\quad 1\leq n\leq Cs,\quad n\in\mathbb N.$$ There is therefore an integer $k_0\geq0$ such that, for every $k\geq k_0$, the first dyadic integer $L_k\in\{1,2,4,\ldots\}$ satisfying $$\label{df:eq:gr-characteristic-threshold}
 \mathbb P(E(s_k,L_k))\leq q_0$$ exists. These lengths satisfy $$\label{df:eq:gr-characteristic-bounds}
 L_k\leq2^{3k},\qquad \frac{L_k}{2^k}\longrightarrow\infty.$$ Moreover, every dyadic integer $l<L_k$ satisfies $\mathbb P(E(s_k,l))>q_0$.

There is a constant $C_1$, independent of $A,R,J,j,k$, and for each fixed $A,R$ a sequence $\beta_J\to0$, such that the following holds. For every fixed $J\ge1$ and all sufficiently large $k\in\mathcal K$, there is an event $G_{k,J}$ with $$\mathbb P(G_{k,J}^{\mathrm c})\le\beta_J$$ on which the following connections exist simultaneously. For every level $J\le j\le j_*$, every grid interval $$[X,X+l_j]\subset[-(A+4)L,(A+4)L],\qquad X\in l_j\mathbb Z,$$ every real $r_0$ with $\left\lvert r_0\right\rvert\le Rs$, and every pair of lattice vertices $p,q$ on its left and right endpoint columns with $$\left\lvert r(p)-r_0\right\rvert\le1,\qquad\left\lvert r(q)-r_0\right\rvert\le1,$$ there is a path from $p$ to $q$ whose cost is at most $$\label{df:eq:gr-grid-cost}
 m_0l_j+C_1j^2w_j,$$ and whose vertices satisfy $$\label{df:eq:gr-grid-localization}
 X-l_j\le x\le X+2l_j,\qquad
 \left\lvert r-r_0\right\rvert\le C_1j^2w_j.$$ One may take $C_1=K+200\kappa/\lambda$. Thus $C_1$ may depend on $K,\kappa,\lambda$; the sequence $\beta_J$ may depend on $A,R,q_0,\kappa,\lambda$.

For every fixed $A,R,\varepsilon>0$, $$\label{df:eq:gr-variable-endpoints}
 \mathbb P\left(
 \begin{array}{c}
 \text{there is a finite path }P\text{ in }
 \{\left\lvert x\right\rvert\leq AL,\ \left\lvert r\right\rvert\leq Rs\}\\
 \text{with }H_+(P)<-\varepsilon s\text{ or }H_-(P)<-\varepsilon s
 \end{array}\right)\longrightarrow0$$ as $k\to\infty$ through $\mathcal K$. The endpoints of $P$ may be any vertices of the region. The convergence is uniform over lattice translations of the region.

Every chunk, including the remainder, satisfies $$\label{df:wi:basic-defect}
  H(Q)\ge-\eta s-2\eta s M_Q.$$ A full chunk satisfies the stronger bound $$\label{df:wi:full-defect}
  H(Q)\ge\eta s-4\eta s M_Q.$$ If $M_{\rm tot}$ denotes the sum of $M_Q$ over the $N$ full chunks and the remainder of $P$, then $$\label{df:wi:total-defect}
  H(P)\ge(N-1)\eta s-4\eta s M_{\rm tot}.$$ The path contains $M_{\rm tot}$ pairwise edge-disjoint witnesses, assigned to the corresponding chunk indices, even if some indices repeat.

There are constants $C,c>0$ such that, for $x,y\in\mathbb Z^2$ and $R=|x-y|_1\ge2$, $$\begin{aligned}
 0\le \mathbb ET(x,y)-\mu(y-x)
     &\le C\sqrt{R\log(2+R)},\label{model:mean}\\
 \mathbb P\bigl(|T(x,y)-\mathbb ET(x,y)|\ge t\sqrt R\bigr)
     &\le C e^{-ct},\qquad t\ge0.\label{model:concentration}
\end{aligned}$$ Consequently, for any fixed $p,C_0<\infty$ and $\alpha\in(1/2,1)$, with probability tending to one all pairs in $[-n^p,n^p]^2\cap\mathbb Z^2$ at distance at most $C_0n$ satisfy $$|T(x,y)-\mu(y-x)|\le n^\alpha.$$ The expected maximum lower deficit over those pairs is at most $C_{p,C_0}\sqrt n\log(2+n)$. The same conclusions hold for metric-graph endpoints after adding an $O(\log n)$ term in expectation.

Let $(\eta_z)_{z\in\mathbb Z^2}$ be independent random elements and let $V_{z,\lambda}\in L^1$, where $\lambda$ belongs to an arbitrary label set with a distinguished label $0$. Set $\mathcal F_I=\sigma(\eta_z:z\in I)$ and $Q_H(z)=z+[-H,H]^2\cap\mathbb Z^2$, with integer $H\geq2$. Suppose that whenever $|z-z'|_1\leq1$, and for every pair of labels in question, there is an $\mathcal F_{Q_H(z)}$-measurable variable $E_{(z,\lambda),(z',\lambda')}$ such that $$\left\|E_{(z,\lambda),(z',\lambda')}
       -V_{z',\lambda'}+V_{z,\lambda}\right\|_1
 \leq\varepsilon.                              \label{found:eq-abstract-hyp}$$ Then there are random variables $g_{z,\lambda}$, measurable with respect to $\mathcal F_{Q_{R+2H}(z)}$, where $R=8H+20$, such that $$\begin{aligned}
 \mathbb Eg_{z,\lambda}&=\mathbb EV_{z,\lambda},\label{found:eq-mean}\\
 \left\|g_{z',\lambda'}-g_{z,\lambda}
       -V_{z',\lambda'}+V_{z,\lambda}\right\|_1
 &\leq C_H\varepsilon.                         \label{found:eq-potential}
\end{aligned}$$ The constant $C_H$ depends only on $H$, not on the number of labels. Translation covariance is preserved whenever the true increment field $V_{z',\lambda'}-V_{z,\lambda}$ is covariant under measure-preserving input shifts and the deterministic means have the required covariance.

Let $(U_z)$ be nonnegative fields whose dependence range is uniform in a possible law index $n$, and suppose $\sup_{n,z}\mathbb P(U_z>t)\le p(t)$. Then, for $T\ge0$, $$\begin{aligned}
 \mathbb E\sup_{\pi\in\mathcal P_L}
       \sum_{z\in\pi}U_z\mathbf1_{\{U_z>T\}}
 &\le C(L+1)\left[T\sqrt{p(T)}+
                         \int_T^\infty\sqrt{p(t)}\,dt\right].
 \label{probability:tail-integral}
\end{aligned}$$ In particular, a uniform bound $\mathbb P(U_z>t)\le A t^{-4}$ for $t\ge1$ gives $C_A(L+1)/T$ for $T\ge1$. The same bounds hold for the site lists and bounded multiplicities in Lemma <a href="#probability:sparse" data-reference-type="ref" data-reference="probability:sparse">34</a>.

Assume <a href="#probability:collection-assumption" data-reference-type="eqref" data-reference="probability:collection-assumption">[probability:collection-assumption]</a>, and fix $H,c,m_0$. For $N\ge1$, let $\mathcal S(N,\delta)$ consist of finite site sequences satisfying <a href="#probability:approximate-order" data-reference-type="eqref" data-reference="probability:approximate-order">[probability:approximate-order]</a> and <a href="#probability:thin-box" data-reference-type="eqref" data-reference="probability:thin-box">[probability:thin-box]</a>, with at most $m_0$ occurrences per site. Suppose also that their distinct sites can be visited by a path whose initial distance from the origin plus total length is at most $c(N+1)$.*


*For every $F\ge1$ there is $C_F<\infty$ such that, for $T\ge1$ and $0\le\delta\le F^{-2}$, $$\mathbb E\sup_{S\in\mathcal S(N,\delta)}
       \sum_{z\in S}Y_z\mathbf1_{\{Y_z>T\}}
 \le \frac{CN}{F}+\frac{C_F(N+1)}{T}+C_F.
 \label{probability:thin-tail-bound}$$ Here $C$ is independent of $F,T,\delta,n$. Consequently, for every $\varepsilon>0$ there are $\delta_0>0$ and $T_0<\infty$ such that, for every sequence of laws and every $N(n)\to\infty$, $$\limsup_{n\to\infty}\frac1{N(n)}
 \mathbb E\sup_{S\in\mathcal S(N(n),\delta_0)}
       \sum_{z\in S}Y_z\mathbf1_{\{Y_z>T_0\}}
 \le\varepsilon.$$ Finite sums of field types and independence sublattices satisfy the same conclusion if their number and all required constants are uniform.

There is a local random variable $W_c$, with the same support as $U_c$, such that $$\label{access:all-conditionals}
 \mathbb E[U_c\mid\mathcal F_{\mathcal D}]\le W_c,
 \qquad \mathbb EW_c\le 2^{m_0}C\beta.$$ The inequalities hold simultaneously for every deterministic union-cell observation $\mathcal D$.

For the stationary local fields and compatible physical pieces constructed above, assume <a href="#improvement:primary:eq2" data-reference-type="eqref" data-reference="improvement:primary:eq2">[improvement:primary:eq2]</a>, <a href="#improvement:transitions:eq3" data-reference-type="eqref" data-reference="improvement:transitions:eq3">[improvement:transitions:eq3]</a>–<a href="#improvement:transitions:eq4" data-reference-type="eqref" data-reference="improvement:transitions:eq4">[improvement:transitions:eq4]</a>, and the stated independence at distances greater than $R$. There is a stationary finite-range rule selecting disjoint replacement intervals. It concatenates primary pieces, successful transitions, and spare pieces. Charge each retained primary cost to its own slot and each replacement’s total normalized corrected cost to its leftmost flag. The resulting integrable local charge $Z_i^{\mathrm{out}}$ satisfies $$\mathbb E Z_0^{\mathrm{out}}
 \le \mathbb EW_0+H_*Mp
 +C_0\{2G_*p+2(L_*+1)\delta\}
 +(H_*C_V+2K_\delta)p.
 \label{improvement:flags:eq8}$$ The range of the rule depends on $R$ and $T_*$, independently of $M$, $\delta$, and $K_\delta$.
